# Connecting 3CX SMS Chat

A complete walkthrough for setting up **inbound + outbound SMS** between your 3CX phone system and any **3CX-compatible SMS provider** — so customers can text your business number and your team replies from the 3CX Web Client chat panel.

> **What this gives you:** customers SMS your business number → the message arrives in 3CX as a chat conversation routed to a queue → an agent replies in 3CX → the reply goes back to the customer's mobile.
>
> **Three places to configure:**
> 1. **3CX Admin Console** — SIP trunk + DID + SMS settings + chat queue routing
> 2. **Your SMS provider's portal** (any 3CX-compatible SMS service — Tronic Cloud, Twilio, Telnyx, etc.) — issues the API Key + Provider URL
> 3. **3CX Web Client** — where agents handle the SMS conversations
>
> This guide is **provider-agnostic**. The 3CX configuration is identical regardless of which SMS service you use — only the provider-portal screens in Part B differ. Your provider must support **3CX's Generic SMS protocol**.

---

## Prerequisites

- A 3CX PBX with admin access to **Admin → SIP Trunks** and **Admin → Call Queues**
- An account with an SMS provider that supports 3CX's Generic SMS protocol, with a provisioned SMS-capable phone number (DID)
- Access to the 3CX Web Client for at least one user / queue agent
- The phone number you want to receive SMS on, in **E.164 format without the `+`** (e.g. `61488883111`)

> **Why no `+`?** 3CX's DID matcher is exact-string. If the carrier sends `to: 61488883111` and your DID is stored as `+61488883111`, 3CX matches **nothing** and silently drops the message with Event ID 30045 in the event log. Always store the DID without the `+`.

---

## Part A — 3CX Admin Console

### Step 1 — Open or create the SIP trunk

1. Log into your 3CX Admin Console (e.g. `https://your-3cx.example.com`).
2. Go to **Admin → SIP Trunks**.
3. Either edit an existing SIP trunk you'll use for SMS, or click **Add SIP Trunk**.
4. On the **General** tab give the trunk a friendly name (e.g. `SMS Provider`).

> **Can SMS share a trunk with voice calls?** Yes, as long as the same trunk also receives voice. But it's cleaner to dedicate one trunk to SMS so the SMS settings don't interfere with voice routing — especially when voice and SMS use different providers.

### Step 2 — Add the DID number

1. Click the **DID Numbers** tab.
2. Click **Add**.
3. Enter your SMS phone number **without the `+`**:
   - ✅ Correct: `61488883111`
   - ❌ Wrong: `+61488883111` or `0488883111`
4. Click **OK**.

The DID stored here must **exactly match** the `to` field the provider sends. Any mismatch = silent drop.

### Step 3 — Configure the SMS tab (first pass)

1. Click the **SMS** tab on the same SIP trunk.
2. Tick **Enable SMS** ✅.
3. The **Webhook URL** will auto-generate. Copy it — it looks like:

```
https://your-3cx.example.com/sms/generic/<random-token>
```

You'll paste this into your SMS provider's portal in Part B.

4. Leave **API Key** and **Provider URL** blank for now — you'll fill them in after Part B.
5. **Caller-ID Number Matching** (optional): if ticked, 3CX tries to match the inbound caller's mobile to an existing 3CX contact. Leave at the default (3 digits) unless you have a specific contact-matching reason.

> If **Enable SMS** is greyed out: you haven't added a DID yet. Go back to Step 2.

### Step 4 — Route inbound SMS to a queue or extension

3CX needs to know which queue / extension should be alerted when an SMS arrives.

**Option A — Route to a queue (recommended for teams)**

1. **Admin → Call Queues** → open or create the queue that handles SMS (e.g. `SMS ONLY - 817`).
2. **Agents** tab: add the extensions that should receive SMS conversations.
3. In the queue settings, ensure **Chat is enabled**.

**Option B — Route directly to an extension**

1. **Admin → Extensions** → open the extension.
2. **Options** tab: ensure chat / messaging is enabled.

**How 3CX picks the destination**: 3CX reads the `to` DID number on the inbound SMS, finds the matching DID on this trunk, then routes to the queue / extension you've configured as the SMS destination. If you need different DIDs to ring different queues, give each queue its own DID.

### Step 5 — Save the trunk (don't close it yet)

Click **OK / Save** on the SIP trunk. Keep this browser tab open — you'll come back to it in Part C.

---

## Part B — Your SMS provider's portal

The exact button names and screen layout differ per provider, but every 3CX-compatible SMS provider follows the same pattern: you give them your 3CX webhook URL, and they give you back an **API Key** and a **Provider URL**.

### Step 6 — Create the SMS integration in your provider's portal

1. Log into your SMS provider's portal at the URL they gave you (e.g. `https://your-provider.example.com`).
2. Navigate to the section for **SMS Integrations**, **3CX SMS**, **Generic SMS Webhook**, or similarly named (varies per provider).
3. Click **New Integration** / **Add** / **Create** (or edit an existing entry if you already have one for this DID).
4. Fill in:

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| **Integration Name** | A friendly label, e.g. `Acme 3CX SMS` |
| **3CX Webhook URL** | The URL you copied from Part A Step 3 |

5. Click **Save**. The provider's portal will display two values you'll need next:

| Field | Example shape |
|---|---|
| **API Key** | `87530f93-d60d-4190-9dc9-147a604e4a1d` |
| **Provider URL** | `https://your-provider.example.com/api/3cx/<uuid>` |

Copy both.

6. Set **Integration Status → Active** (or equivalent — some providers enable automatically on save).

> **Don't share these values.** The API Key authenticates 3CX's outbound replies into the SMS carrier. Anyone with the key + Provider URL can send SMS through your account.
>
> **If your provider's terminology differs**: the API Key may be called "Token", "Auth Key", or "Webhook Secret". The Provider URL may be called "Outbound Endpoint", "Webhook URL", or "Carrier URL". The values are functionally the same — paste them into the matching 3CX field.

---

## Part C — Back to 3CX Admin

### Step 7 — Complete the SMS tab

1. Return to the SIP trunk you saved in Step 5 → **SMS** tab.
2. Paste the values from Step 6:
   - **API Key** → the API Key from your provider's portal
   - **Provider URL** → the Provider URL from your provider's portal
3. Click **Save / Apply**.

The trunk is now **bidirectionally** connected:
- **Inbound**: the carrier POSTs incoming SMS to 3CX's Webhook URL.
- **Outbound**: 3CX POSTs replies to the Provider URL, authenticated with the API Key.

---

## Part D — 3CX Web Client (Agent Side)

### Step 8 — Receiving inbound SMS

When a customer texts your number:

1. Open the **3CX Web Client** (e.g. `https://your-3cx.example.com/webclient`).
2. Click the **Chat** icon in the left sidebar.
3. The inbound SMS appears as a new conversation under the queue name (e.g. `SMS ONLY (817)`).
4. The conversation shows:
   - **From**: customer's mobile number (e.g. `+61416059519`)
   - **Channel indicator**: SMS
5. All agents in the queue are notified per the queue's notification settings.

### Step 9 — Replying from 3CX

1. Click the conversation in the chat list.
2. Type your reply in the message box at the bottom.
3. Press **Enter** or click **Send**.
4. 3CX POSTs the reply to the **Provider URL** (Step 7), the carrier delivers it to the customer's mobile.

> **Outgoing-message detail**: in the carrier's outbound payload, `from` is the DID number (`+61488883111`) and `to` is the customer's mobile. If a 3CX contact record exists for the customer, the `to` field may carry the contact name instead of the raw number — keep this in mind if you process replies programmatically.

---

## How it all connects

```
Customer sends SMS to +61488883111
         ↓
SMS provider's carrier receives it
         ↓
Provider POSTs to 3CX Webhook URL  ← configured in provider portal (Part B)
         ↓
3CX matches DID (61488883111) → routes to queue (e.g. 817 / SMS ONLY)
         ↓
Agent sees conversation in 3CX Web Client → Chat
         ↓
Agent replies → 3CX POSTs to Provider URL  ← configured in 3CX SMS tab (Part C)
         ↓
Provider delivers SMS reply to customer's mobile
```

